+    // `setClientRect` is only ever reached from a draw pass (RenderableView.draw,
+    // GroupView.drawGroup, ImageView, UseView, ForeignObjectView). Dispatching a
+    // Fabric event from in there re-enters the mounting layer mid-draw: Reanimated
+    // hooks event dispatch and answers it by flushing its whole pending
+    // synchronous prop buffer (NodesManager.onEventDispatch ->
+    // performOperationsRespectingDrawPass -> NativeProxy.synchronouslyUpdateUIProps
+    // -> MountingManager.updatePropsSynchronously). Any buffered entry whose view
+    // is no longer registered in a SurfaceMountingManager then throws
+    // RetryableMountingLayerException, which Reanimated catches and logs with a
+    // full stack trace - on the main thread, inside View.draw(). Measured on a
+    // Galaxy A33: 250-400 of those per second during a screen transition, followed
+    // by 48-82 skipped frames, with the dropped prop updates never re-sent
+    // (Reanimated only ships changed props).
+    //
+    // The layout above must stay synchronous - it feeds touch handling in this
+    // same frame - so only the event is deferred. `post` runs it after the draw
+    // pass on the same (main) thread, which is where an onLayout event belongs
+    // anyway; RN dispatches its own onLayout asynchronously to JS for the same
+    // reason. The dispatcher is re-resolved inside the runnable so a view that was
+    // detached in the meantime dispatches nothing instead of aiming at a dead
+    // surface, and the pending runnable is coalesced so a resize storm queues one
+    // event carrying the latest rect rather than one per intermediate rect.
+    if (mPendingLayoutEvent != null) {
+      removeCallbacks(mPendingLayoutEvent);
     }
